====RULES
181:# The task lifecycle
182-
183-For every new task:
184-
185-1. Create a GitHub issue describing the task before starting work.
186-2. Develop on a branch; reference that issue number in commit messages (e.g. `Refs #123`,
187-   `Fixes #123`, or `Closes #123`).
188-3. Open the PR with a closing keyword for that issue on its own line in the **body** —
189-   `Closes #123`. That is what fills GitHub's *Development* panel, the link between PR and issue
190-   that anyone browsing either one follows; `Refs #123` is a cross-reference and links nothing
191-   there. A PR that must not close its issue — one slice of a longer plan, one of several against
192-   a tracker — still links: give the slice its own issue and close that one.
193-4. Update the issue's status (comments / close) as work progresses and when it's done.
194-
195-- **Spotting a change that should wait until the work in flight lands** — file it as work that
196-  comes back on its own rather than doing it now or trusting anyone to remember it: the
197-  [do-later](skills/do-later/SKILL.md) skill, which queues it behind what it waits on.
198-
199-- **Filing anything into the ad-hoc queue** (a deferral, a verification, any marked issue) — it
200-  asks an unattended session **on this repository** to do the work, so only file what such a
201-  session can actually do here. A read of another repository or a console is not work it can do:
202-  that item parks minutes after it is picked, and a park is a person's problem filed under a
203-  mechanism's name. A public URL is the one exception, through the coded verification form
